## Context

The AIDE methodology is a doc set — the canonical files under `docs/`
— and before this helper existed, the only delivery mechanism for those
files was to concatenate them into a single block inside the host's
config file. That worked while the methodology was small, but the
methodology's own doctrine is progressive disclosure: agents should
read only the part of a doc set their current task actually requires.
Shipping the whole set into a surface the agent reads on every session
was the opposite of the pattern the docs were teaching.

This helper exists to install the methodology the right way: as a
folder of canonical docs sitting on the host project's disk, one file
per canonical name, with a hub index file that names all of them. An
agent following a pointer from the host's config file reads the hub
index first, sees the full list, and crawls into only the specific doc
its task needs. The pattern mirrors what `study-playbook` already does
against the user's global brain — a shallow index that enumerates a
deep set.

The parent spec's chokepoint invariant governs this addition: every
read of `docs/` within the install service subtree goes through
initContent. Adding a second reader would be a regression even if
the local code were simpler for it, because the single-reader
invariant is what guarantees that a future change to `docs/` is a
one-file edit. This helper participates in that invariant by asking
initContent both for the content of each methodology doc and for the
list of which docs belong in the methodology set.

## Strategy

**The host-side hub mirrors the canonical shape, flat and verbatim.**
Every canonical methodology doc is written to the host under its own
canonical-name file, with its bytes unmodified from the source in
`.aide/docs/`. There is no rewriting of headings, no stripping of
frontmatter, no link rewriting, no composition. A file diff between
the installed hub and the canonical source is empty: every file in
the host hub is byte-identical to its source in `.aide/docs/`,
index included. The moment this helper becomes "smart" about the
canonical docs, it becomes a second source of truth and begins to
drift on the first edit upstream.

**The hub index is canonical, not composed.** The installed hub
needs a file the agent lands on first when it follows the stub's
pointer — an index that names and links to every doc in the hub.
That index lives in `.aide/docs/index.md` alongside its siblings,
gets enumerated by initContent like every other methodology doc,
and is written verbatim by the main install loop. Composing it in
source would make the index a second surface the maintainer has to
touch for any hub-level edit; keeping it canonical means a single
file edit in the repo propagates to every future host install.

**Enumeration lives in initContent, not here.** The list of which
canonical docs belong to the methodology set is the kind of decision
that must live alongside the name-to-path registry initContent
already owns. If a canonical doc is added or removed, the registry
change and the methodology-membership change are the same change, and
they land in the same file. This helper asks initContent for the
enumeration at call time and iterates whatever list comes back.
Hardcoding the list locally would double the surface at which a
canonical-doc change has to land, and the second landing site would
always be the one that got missed.

**Per-file idempotency, per-file reporting.** The pattern is exactly
the one scaffoldCommands already uses for the pipeline slash
commands: iterate the list, check whether the target file exists,
either write it and report `created` or skip it and report `exists`.
No aggregation, no fail-fast, no silent overwrite. The init summary
renders the per-file statuses directly. This design is a conscious
clone of a helper that already works; the consistency across
installers matters more than any micro-optimization either could make
on its own.

**Target path derived from a shared source.** The host-side hub path
and the path writeMethodology names in its stub must agree by
construction. This helper and writeMethodology both read the path
from a single shared constant or helper — whichever shape the
architect chooses — so that editing the path in one place updates
both the installer and the stub simultaneously. The invariant is
"stub-points-at-hub"; the mechanism is shared state. Any other
arrangement — copy-paste string literals, helper-private defaults,
caller-supplied paths the two sides don't consult together — is a
design regression because it admits the possibility of drift.

**Independent failures, not cascade failures.** Each doc install is
its own best-effort operation. An unreadable canonical doc fails
that one install, surfaces in the per-file status as an error, and
leaves the other installs running. This mirrors how the parent
install service already treats IDE configuration: a missing optional
dependency is a skip, not a run-ending exception. A developer whose
install produced four successful doc installs and one failed one
still has an almost-complete hub and a clear report of what is
missing; a developer whose install exploded on the first failure
has nothing at all.

## Good examples

A host project runs the install action for the first time — invoked
through `aide_init` from a connected agent or via `npx aidemd-mcp@latest
init` from the terminal. The helper asks initContent for the list of
canonical methodology docs, receives eight names (index plus seven
methodology docs), and iterates them. For each name it asks
initContent for the doc's bytes, writes those bytes to the
corresponding host-side file under the shared doc-hub path, and
reports `created`. The install summary shows a `created` line per
canonical doc for this step alongside the methodology stub, the
scaffolded slash commands, and the MCP wiring. The developer opens
the host-side hub folder and sees the canonical doc set whose
contents are byte-identical to the docs the maintainer edits in
`.aide/docs/`.

A developer re-runs the install action on an already-initialized
project — through either entry point. Every doc install checks the
target, finds the file present, and reports `exists`. The hub index
reports `exists` as well. Nothing on disk changed; the summary reads
cleanly as "already done"; the developer's time spent on the re-run
was zero.

A developer has customized one of the installed methodology docs to
add their team's interpretation of a rule. They re-run the install
action to pick up a change to the MCP wiring. The helper sees the
customized doc in place and reports `exists`, leaving the
customization intact. The other four docs also report `exists`. The
customization survived the re-run by the same idempotency rule that
protects scaffolded slash commands.

A maintainer adds a new canonical methodology doc to `docs/` and
extends initContent's enumeration API to include it. They release a
new version of the server. On the next host-project install, this
helper iterates the updated list without any code change of its own,
installs the new doc alongside the seven existing ones, and the hub
index now includes a link to the new doc. The change landed in one
file (initContent's registry); this helper picked it up for free;
the host project gained the new methodology surface without any
developer intervention beyond running the install action.

An agent session in a host project needs to scaffold a new `.aide`
file. Following the stub's pointer, it lands on the hub index, sees
a link to `aide-spec.md` and another to `aide-template.md`, and
reads only those two. It does not read `automated-qa.md` because
the current task is not a QA loop. The session spent the token budget
on the specific material its task needed, not on the full methodology,
which is exactly the progressive disclosure this helper was designed
to enable at the delivery layer.

## Bad examples

A helper that reads `docs/aide-spec.md` directly with `readFile`
"because it's one more line than calling into initContent". The
single-reader invariant has just been broken at this helper. A later
rename of `aide-spec.md` updates the initContent registry and breaks
this helper because nothing on this side is listening; the init run
on the next host project fails with a file-not-found error that
points at the wrong module.

A helper that hardcodes a local array of methodology doc names —
`["aide-spec", "aide-template", "plan-aide", "todo-aide",
"progressive-disclosure", "agent-readable-code", "automated-qa"]`
— inside its own source. A maintainer adds an eighth canonical doc
to `docs/` and extends the initContent registry. The registry
propagates through writeMethodology on the next build, but this
helper silently continues installing seven docs, and the hub index
silently continues listing seven entries. The new canonical doc
exists in the registry and is invisible to host projects. The
enumeration invariant has been broken by the second source of truth
for what counts as "the methodology".

A helper that catches a read error on one canonical doc and aborts
the whole install, returning zero files written. The host project
has no methodology hub at all, the init summary shows a single
aggregate failure, and the developer has to debug the helper before
they can even read what *should* have been installed. Per-file
independence was the whole point of the design; skipping it traded
it for single-exception simplicity that doesn't belong in an
init tool.

A helper that detects an existing doc in the host hub, compares its
content against the canonical source, and overwrites it when the two
differ "because the canonical is authoritative". The developer's
customization is gone, they notice only when their next AIDE-related
task produces the wrong suggestion, and trust in the tool is broken.
The idempotency rule is "detect and skip", not "detect and
reconcile".

A helper that writes its doc hub to one path and a matching stub
that names a slightly different path — `aide/` versus `.aide/`,
trailing slash versus no trailing slash, forward slash versus
platform-native separator. The stub points at nothing; the agent
lands on a missing directory; the refactor technically shipped and
is technically broken. A shared path source would have caught this
at compile time; two coincident literals did not catch it at all.

A helper that composes the hub index in source instead of reading
`.aide/docs/index.md` through initContent. A maintainer edits the
canonical index file in the repo expecting the edit to ship; the
next host init runs the composer, writes its own version on top,
and the maintainer's edit is invisible to every host project. The
index is a canonical doc like every other file under `.aide/docs/`;
composing it anywhere else is a second source of truth for the same
content.
